 "overview": {
  "summary": "The instrument's unguarded single-board computer, and the largest assembly in it -- five schematic sheets against nobody else's four. A 68HC000 at U8 running from a 7.3728 MHz crystal, 512 KB of EPROM in four sockets, 128 KB of static RAM in four more, a 32 KB EEPROM at U13 holding the calibration constants, a DUART at U31 for the fibre-optic link to the guarded digital section, and a battery-backed clock/calendar at U33. Around them sit the support circuits that decide whether any of it runs: a power-up and reset circuit built on line monitor U1, a watchdog timer, an address decoder and DTACK generator in three programmed logic devices, and a priority interrupt controller. It generates no supply of its own -- +5 V and ±12 V arrive from the Digital Power Supply (A19) through P61 and P62 -- and its only local source of power is BT1, the 3 V lithium cell that keeps the clock running with the instrument off. The board is drawn with more positions than it is built with: three ROM socket pairs of which two are filled, three RAM socket pairs of which two are filled, a second development DUART and its RS-232 connector J5, and an alternate oscillator Y2, all left empty on production units and all labelled as such on the sheets.",
  "connectors": "P61 (64-pin DIN 41612 type C) carries the front panel interface and the two fan-monitor sense lines; P62 (the same part) carries the rear panel interface and the guard-crossing serial link TXDB, RCVB and XMT. Both mate with the Digital Motherboard (A4). J5, a 12-pin header for the development DUART's RS-232 channel, is not installed.",
  "source": "§2-30 to §2-45, Tables 2-3, 2-4 and 2-5, and Figure 7-24 sheets 1-5"
 },
